 "answer": "Singapore's air quality is currently poor. The most recent readings I found are from the morning and midday of Friday, 16 October 2026. I can't pull a live feed, so the numbers may have moved since then.\n\n**Latest readings**\n- The 24-hour PSI across the five regions was 153–226, which is \"very unhealthy\". The highest was in Central. This was the NEA reading at 12pm on 16 Oct, as reported by a third-party tracker.\n- Earlier in the day, the 24-hour PSI in central Singapore hit 227 at 9am, a new high for the year. By 10am the east and west had also entered the very unhealthy band, at 202 and 201.\n- The highest 1-hour PM2.5 reading was 194 µg/m³, in the \"High\" band.\n\n**Background**\n- Singapore has had unhealthy PSI readings every day since Oct 5. That is the longest continuous stretch since the 2015 haze crisis.\n- The headline is about action against companies responsible for forest fires, which points to a regional haze episode.\n\n**What this means**\n- A 24-hour PSI above 200 is \"very unhealthy\". During these periods, the NEA generally advises everyone to avoid prolonged or strenuous outdoor activity. Children, the elderly and people with heart or lung conditions should be especially careful.\n- For short-term plans like a jog or a trip outside, check the 1-hour PM2.5 reading.\n\nFor the current figures, check the NEA's site (haze.gov.sg) or the myENV app.",
